.TH SWIFT-RING-MANAGER "1" "July 2026" "OpenStack Swift"

.SH NAME
swift\-ring\-manager \- manage a Swift ring-manager service over HTTP

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swift\-ring\-manager
[\fIglobal-options\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIcommand-options\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B swift-ring-manager
is an operator CLI for the Swift ring-manager control-plane API.
It manages ring metadata and builder-backed device membership, and reports
ring analysis.
It talks to a running
.B swift-ring-manager-server
and follows its read and write restrictions.
Mutating commands must target a server in
.B primary
mode.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
Ring-manager base URL.
If omitted, the tool uses \fBSWIFT_RING_MANAGER_URL\fR or
\fBRING_MANAGER_URL\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-admin-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
Value to send in \fBX-Ring-Manager-Admin-Key\fR.
The environment alternatives are \fBSWIFT_RING_MANAGER_ADMIN_KEY\fR and
\fBRING_MANAGER_ADMIN_KEY\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-admin-key-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
File containing the admin key.
.TP
\fB\-\-read-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
Value to send in \fBX-Ring-Manager-Read-Key\fR for read requests.
.TP
\fB\-\-read-key-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
File containing the read key.
.TP
\fB\-\-auth-token\fR \fITOKEN\fR
Value to send in \fBX-Auth-Token\fR for requests using the admin credential.
.TP
\fB\-\-read-auth-token\fR \fITOKEN\fR
Value to send in \fBX-Auth-Token\fR for requests using the read credential.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
HTTP request timeout.
The default is 30 seconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print complete command results as JSON.
List commands print compact tables by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry-run\fR
For mutating commands, print the HTTP method, path, and body without sending
the request.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBstatus\fR
Show ring-manager service status.
.TP
\fBrings list\fR
List logical Swift rings.
.TP
\fBrings show\fR \fIRING_ID\fR
Show one logical Swift ring.
.TP
.B rings build
.I RING_ID
[\fB\-\-seed\fR \fISEED\fR]
[\fB\-\-format-version\fR 1|2]
Queue one immutable per-ring artifact build without creating a cluster release
or changing the top-level \fBlatest\fR release pointer.
This is also available for disabled rings.
When \fB\-\-format-version\fR is omitted, ring-manager writes v1 for builders
whose device IDs fit in the legacy 2-byte ring format and v2 for builders with
wider device IDs.
Explicit \fB\-\-format-version 1\fR fails for builders that require wider IDs.
.TP
\fBrings create\fR [ring field options]
Create a logical Swift ring.
Common fields include \fB\-\-type\fR, \fB\-\-policy-index\fR,
\fB\-\-policy-type\fR, \fB\-\-part-power\fR, \fB\-\-replicas\fR,
\fB\-\-min-part-hours\fR, \fB\-\-builder-file\fR, \fB\-\-disabled\fR,
and \fB\-\-enabled\fR.
.TP
\fBrings update\fR \fIRING_ID\fR [\fB\-\-replace\fR] [ring field options]
Patch a logical Swift ring.
With \fB\-\-replace\fR, use PUT instead of PATCH.
.TP
\fBrings delete\fR \fIRING_ID\fR \fB\-\-confirm\fR
Delete a logical Swift ring metadata record.
The explicit confirmation prevents an accidental destructive invocation.
.TP
\fBrings part-power\fR \fIprepare|increase|cancel|finish\fR \fIRING_ID\fR
Run one step in an object-ring partition-power-increase lifecycle.
Publishing and relinking remain separate operator actions.
.TP
\fBdevices list\fR \fIRING_ID\fR
List builder device records for a ring.
.TP
\fBdevices add\fR \fIRING_ID\fR [\fB\-\-from-file\fR \fIFILE\fR|\fIDEVICE_SPEC WEIGHT\fR ...]
Add builder device records from a JSON or YAML inventory, or from
swift-ring-builder-style shorthand.
.TP
\fBdevices remove\fR \fIRING_ID\fR [\fB\-\-from-file\fR \fIFILE\fR|\fIDEVICE_SELECTOR\fR ...]
Mark matching builder devices for removal.
Selectors use the same shorthand accepted by \fBswift-ring-builder\fR, such
as \fBd0\fR or \fB/sdb\fR.
.TP
\fBdevices replace\fR \fIRING_ID\fR [\fB\-\-from-file\fR \fIFILE\fR|\fIDEVICE_SPEC WEIGHT\fR ...]
Replace the active builder device set for a ring.
.TP
\fBanalyze parts|rebalance|dispersion|at_risk|count_parts\fR \fIRING_ID\fR
Request the matching read-only analysis endpoint.
\fBanalyze dispersion\fR accepts \fB\-\-level\fR.
\fBanalyze count_parts\fR accepts repeated \fB\-\-replication-ip\fR values
and \fB\-\-risk-count\fR.
.TP
\fBanalyze partitions_at_risk\fR \fIRING_ID\fR [selectors]
Check a down-node or down-device scenario without modifying a builder.
Use repeated \fB\-\-node-ip\fR, \fB\-\-replication-ip\fR, or
\fB\-\-device-id\fR options, or pass a JSON or YAML selector body with
\fB\-\-from-file\fR.
Use \fB\-\-details\fR to include partition IDs.

.TP
.B versions list
List published immutable cluster releases.
.TP
.B versions publish
[\fB\-\-version\fR \fIVERSION\fR]
[\fB\-\-ring\fR \fIRING_ID\fR]
[\fB\-\-seed\fR \fISEED\fR]
[\fB\-\-format-version\fR 1|2]
Build a complete enabled-ring release manifest.
\fB\-\-ring\fR may be repeated to select the rings to rebuild.
Other enabled rings are carried forward from their latest per-ring artifact.
Release version IDs cannot be reused.
The command returns a queued build job; use \fBbuilds show\fR to inspect it.
When \fB\-\-format-version\fR is omitted, ring-manager writes v1 for builders
whose device IDs fit in the legacy 2-byte ring format and v2 for builders with
wider device IDs.
Explicit \fB\-\-format-version 1\fR fails for builders that require wider IDs.
.TP
.B builds list
[\fB\-\-retry-of\fR \fIBUILD_ID\fR] [\fB\-\-retry-root\fR \fIBUILD_ID\fR]
List persistent ring build jobs.
\fB\-\-retry-of\fR selects direct retries from one source build.
\fB\-\-retry-root\fR selects all descendants in one retry chain.
.TP
.B builds show
.I BUILD_ID
Show one persistent ring build job and its result or failure detail.
.TP
.B builds cancel
.I BUILD_ID
[\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIREASON\fR]
Cancel a queued or deferred ring build job.
Already-building, completed, and failed jobs are rejected by the server.
Cancellation does not interrupt an active rebuild or publication attempt.
.TP
.B builds retry
.I BUILD_ID
[\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIREASON\fR]
Retry a failed or cancelled build as a fresh queued job.
The original job remains terminal history and the new job records its retry
lineage.
.TP
.B versions show
[\fIVERSION\fR]
Show one release record.
Defaults to \fBlatest\fR.
.TP
.B versions latest
Show the latest published release.
.TP
.B versions manifest
[\fIVERSION\fR]
Show one release manifest.
Defaults to \fBlatest\fR.
.TP
.B versions download
[\fIVERSION\fR]
\fB\-\-output-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Download every artifact in a release after verifying byte counts and SHA-256
checksums.

.SH DEVICE INVENTORIES
.PP
Device add and replace accept a direct API payload with a top-level
\fBdevices\fR list, or an inventory with a top-level \fBnodes\fR list.
Node inventories expand each disk into an explicit Swift device record.
See \fIetc/ring-manager-devices.yaml-sample\fR for an example.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Show status:
.IP
.nf
swift-ring-manager --url http://ring.example.com:6205 status
.fi
.TP
Create an object ring:
.IP
.nf
swift-ring-manager --url http://ring.example.com:6205 \\
  rings create --type object --policy-index 0 \\
  --policy-type replication --part-power 18 --replicas 3 \\
  --min-part-hours 1 --builder-file object.builder
.fi
.TP
Add a device using ring-builder shorthand:
.IP
.nf
swift-ring-manager --url http://ring.example.com:6205 \\
  devices add object-0 r1z1-10.0.0.1:6200/sdb 100
.fi
.TP
Check the effect of a failed node:
.IP
.nf
swift-ring-manager --url http://ring.example.com:6205 \\
  analyze partitions_at_risk object-0 --node-ip 10.0.0.1
.fi

.SH DOCUMENTATION
.LP
More documentation about OpenStack Swift is available at
.BI https://docs.openstack.org/swift/latest/
